{"name":"Snow Leopard","page":"Snow_leopard","group":"Mammal","where":"High mountains of Central and South Asia","diet":"Wild sheep and goats, marmots, hares","facts":["Its long, thick tail is almost as long as its body; it wraps it around its face like a scarf to sleep.","It can leap about 15 meters (50 feet) in one bound.","Unlike other big cats, it can't roar: it chuffs, mews, hisses and wails."],"lesson":"Strength doesn't need to be loud. The snow leopard can't roar, yet it rules the highest mountains on Earth through patience and quiet skill.","about":"The snow leopard is a felid species in the genus Panthera. It has a thick whitish to grey fur with black spots on the head and neck, with larger rosettes on the back, flanks and bushy tail. Males are larger than the females, and both sexes have long canine teeth.","description":"Species of large felid","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a5/Irbis4.JPG?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"Irbis1983","license":"Public domain","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Irbis4.JPG"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Snow_leopard","sci":"Panthera uncia","status":"VU","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":1},
{"name":"Axolotl","page":"Axolotl","group":"Amphibian","where":"Only Lake Xochimilco, by Mexico City","diet":"Worms, insects, small fish","facts":["It stays a \"teenager\" its whole life, keeping its feathery gills and living underwater.","It can regrow legs, its tail, parts of its heart and even parts of its brain.","There are far more axolotls in aquariums and labs than left in the wild."],"lesson":"Healing is possible. The axolotl regrows what it loses, a reminder that setbacks don't have to be permanent if you give yourself time to rebuild.","about":"The axolotl is a species of mole salamander. It is neotenic, reaching sexual maturity without undergoing metamorphosis, and the adults remain fully aquatic with obvious external gills. Axolotls may be difficult to distinguish from the larval stage of other neotenic adult mole salamanders, in particular the tiger salamander, or other species such as mudpuppies.","description":"Species of salamander","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/00/Axolotl_ganz.jpg/1920px-Axolotl_ganz.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"LoKiLeCh","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Axolotl_ganz.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Axolotl","sci":"Ambystoma mexicanum","status":"CR","mass":null,"length":0.457,"life":null,"n":2},
{"name":"Peacock Mantis Shrimp","page":"Peacock_mantis_shrimp","group":"Crustacean","where":"Warm reefs of the Indian and Pacific Oceans","diet":"Crabs, snails, clams, fish","facts":["Its punch is so fast it boils the water for an instant and makes a flash of light.","It can break aquarium glass.","Its eyes have up to 16 kinds of color receptors; people have three."],"lesson":"Size isn't power. A creature smaller than your hand throws the fastest punch in nature: what you lack in size, focus can make up.","about":"Odontodactylus scyllarus, commonly known as the peacock mantis shrimp, harlequin mantis shrimp, painted mantis shrimp, clown mantis shrimp, rainbow mantis shrimp, peacock odontodactyl, thumb splitter, or simply mantis shrimp, is a large odontodactylid stomatopod native to the epipelagic seabed across the Indo-Pacific, ranging from the Marianas to East Africa, and as far South as Northern KwaZulu Natal in South Africa.","description":"Species of mantis shrimp","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1e/Odontodactylus_scyllarus_R%C3%A9union.jpg/1920px-Odontodactylus_scyllarus_R%C3%A9union.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Cédric Péneau","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Odontodactylus_scyllarus_R%C3%A9union.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Odontodactylus_scyllarus","sci":"Odontodactylus scyllarus","status":null,"mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":3},
{"name":"Common Octopus","page":"Common_octopus","group":"Mollusc","where":"The eastern Atlantic and the Mediterranean, from tide pools to deep water","diet":"Crabs, shrimp, clams, fish","facts":["It has three hearts and blue blood.","Most of its neurons are in its arms, which can taste what they touch.","It can change its color and skin texture in a fraction of a second, even though it's colorblind."],"lesson":"Adapt instead of resisting. The octopus survives with no shell at all, by changing color, shape and plan the moment things change.","about":"The common octopus is a mollusk belonging to the class Cephalopoda. Octopus vulgaris is one of the most studied of all the octopus species, and also one of the most intelligent. It ranges from the eastern Atlantic, extends from the Mediterranean Sea, Black sea and the southern coast of England, to the southern coast of South Africa.","description":"Species of cephalopod","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/57/Octopus2.jpg/1920px-Octopus2.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"albert kok","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Octopus2.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Common_octopus","sci":"Octopus vulgaris","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":1.5,"n":4},
{"name":"Red Panda","page":"Red_panda","group":"Mammal","where":"Bamboo forests of the eastern Himalayas","diet":"Mostly bamboo leaves, plus fruit and eggs","facts":["It was named \"panda\" first; the giant panda got the name later.","It has a false thumb, a wrist bone that helps it grip bamboo.","It wraps its fluffy tail around itself like a blanket in the cold."],"lesson":"Gentleness is its own kind of survival. The red panda thrives on a diet others ignore, by being patient, careful and content with little.","about":"The red panda, also known as the lesser panda, is a small mammal native to the eastern Himalayas and southwestern China. It has dense reddish-brown fur with a black belly and legs, white-lined ears, a mostly white muzzle and a ringed tail. Its head-to-body length is 51–63.5 cm (20.1–25.0 in) with a 28–48.5 cm (11.0–19.1 in) tail, and it weighs between 3.2 and 15 kg.","description":"Species of mammal in Asia","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fd/Red_Panda%2C_Gentle_Tree-Dweller_of_the_Himalayas.jpg/1920px-Red_Panda%2C_Gentle_Tree-Dweller_of_the_Himalayas.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Sunuwargr","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Red_Panda,_Gentle_Tree-Dweller_of_the_Himalayas.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Red_panda","sci":"Ailurus fulgens","status":"EN","mass":4.95,"length":null,"life":null,"n":5},
{"name":"Peregrine Falcon","page":"Peregrine_falcon","group":"Bird","where":"Every continent except Antarctica","diet":"Other birds, caught in flight","facts":["Diving for prey, it's the fastest animal on Earth: over 320 km/h (200 mph).","Special bony cones in its nostrils help it breathe at top speed.","It nests on skyscrapers and bridges in big cities, including Miami."],"lesson":"Comebacks are real. Peregrines nearly vanished, and now they nest on skyscrapers; the right change at the right time can turn everything around.","about":"The peregrine falcon, sometimes referred to simply as the peregrine, is a bird of prey (raptor) in the family Falconidae known for its speed. A large, crow-sized falcon, it has a blue-grey back, barred white underparts and a black head. As is typical for bird-eating (avivore) raptors, peregrine falcons are sexually dimorphic, with females being considerably larger than males.","description":"Fastest known animal and common bird of prey","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/9c/Falco_peregrinus_m_Humber_Bay_Park_Toronto.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"Mykola Swarnyk","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Falco_peregrinus_m_Humber_Bay_Park_Toronto.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peregrine_falcon","sci":"Falco peregrinus","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":6},
{"name":"West Indian Manatee","page":"West_Indian_manatee","group":"Mammal","where":"Warm coasts and rivers from Florida through the Caribbean to Brazil","diet":"Seagrass and water plants","facts":["Its closest living relatives are elephants.","It replaces its teeth all its life: new molars move forward like a conveyor belt.","It can eat about a tenth of its own weight in plants every day."],"lesson":"Slow is not weak. Manatees move gently, eat plants and have no real predators besides us: calm can be a life well lived.","about":"The West Indian manatee, also known as the North American manatee, is a large, aquatic mammal native to warm coastal areas of the Caribbean, from the Eastern United States to northern Brazil. Living alone or in herds, it feeds on underwater plants and uses its whiskers to navigate.","description":"Species of mammal","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/99/Manatee_with_calf.PD_-_colour_corrected.jpg/1920px-Manatee_with_calf.PD_-_colour_corrected.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Galen Rathbun","license":"Public domain","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Manatee_with_calf.PD_-_colour_corrected.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/West_Indian_manatee","sci":"Trichechus manatus","status":"VU","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":7},
{"name":"Ground Pangolin","page":"Ground_pangolin","group":"Mammal","where":"Savannas and woodlands of eastern and southern Africa","diet":"Ants and termites","facts":["It's one of the only mammals covered in scales, made of keratin like your fingernails.","It walks on its back legs, holding its front feet and tail off the ground for balance.","When scared, it rolls into a ball that even a lion can't open."],"lesson":"Protecting yourself is not the same as attacking. The pangolin's armor lets it curl up and wait out danger without hurting anyone.","about":"The ground pangolin, also known as Temminck's pangolin, Cape pangolin or steppe pangolin is a species of pangolin from genus Smutsia of subfamily Smutsiinae the within family Manidae. It is one of four species of pangolins which can be found in Africa, and the only one in southern and eastern Africa. The animal was named for the Dutch zoologist Coenraad Jacob Temminck.","description":"Species of mammal","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/6b/Manis_temminckii_%2829645803646%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"U.S. Fish and Wildlife Service Headquarters","license":"CC BY 2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Manis_temminckii_(29645803646).jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ground_pangolin","sci":"Smutsia temminckii","status":"VU","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":8},
{"name":"Leafy Seadragon","page":"Leafy_seadragon","group":"Fish","where":"Cool seas off southern Australia","diet":"Tiny shrimp-like animals","facts":["Its leafy flaps are for camouflage only; it swims with nearly invisible fins.","Like seahorses, the father carries the eggs.","It has no teeth or stomach; it slurps food through its tube snout."],"lesson":"Blending in can be wisdom. The seadragon survives by looking like it belongs to its surroundings, not by fighting them.","about":"The leafy seadragon or Glauert's seadragon, is a marine fish. It is the only member of the genus Phycodurus in the family Syngnathidae, which includes seadragons, pipefish, and seahorses.","description":"Species of fish","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e4/Leafy_Seadragon_on_Kangaroo_Island.jpg/1920px-Leafy_Seadragon_on_Kangaroo_Island.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"James Rosindell","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Leafy_Seadragon_on_Kangaroo_Island.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leafy_seadragon","sci":"Phycodurus eques","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":9},
{"name":"Komodo Dragon","page":"Komodo_dragon","group":"Reptile","where":"A few islands in Indonesia","diet":"Deer, pigs, water buffalo, carrion","facts":["It's the largest living lizard, up to 3 meters (10 feet) long.","Its bite delivers venom that keeps blood from clotting.","Females can have babies without a male."],"lesson":"Patience wins long games. The Komodo dragon often waits days after striking, trusting that time is on its side.","about":"The Komodo dragon, also known as the Komodo monitor, is a large reptile of the monitor lizard family Varanidae that is endemic to the Indonesian islands of Komodo, Rinca, Flores, Gili Dasami, and Gili Motang. The largest extant population lives within the Komodo National Park in Eastern Indonesia.","description":"Largest living species of lizard","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f5/202306_Varanus_komodoensis.jpg/1920px-202306_Varanus_komodoensis.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"James Jolokia (james1203)","license":"CC BY 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:202306_Varanus_komodoensis.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Komodo_dragon","sci":"Varanus komodoensis","status":"EN","mass":70.0,"length":1.5,"life":30.0,"n":10},
{"name":"Milnesium tardigradum","page":"Milnesium_tardigradum","group":"Micro-animal","where":"Moss, lichen and damp soil around the world","diet":"Other tiny animals: rotifers, roundworms, even other tardigrades","facts":["It's about half a millimeter long and walks on eight stubby legs.","Dried out, it survived 10 days in the open vacuum of space on a satellite in 2007.","Unlike most tardigrades, it's a hunter, with a mouth made for grabbing prey and sucking it dry."],"lesson":"Resilience is quiet. The tardigrade survives what would destroy anything else by pausing, conserving, and coming back when conditions improve.","about":"Milnesium tardigradum is a cosmopolitan species of tardigrade that can be found in a diverse range of environments. It has also been found in the sea around Antarctica. M. tardigradum was described by Louis Michel François Doyère in 1840.","description":"Species of tardigrade","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/cd/SEM_image_of_Milnesium_tardigradum_in_active_state_-_journal.pone.0045682.g001-2.png?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"Schokraie E, Warnken U, Hotz-Wagenblatt A, Grohme MA, Hengherr S, et al. (2012)","license":"CC BY 2.5","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:SEM_image_of_Milnesium_tardigradum_in_active_state_-_journal.pone.0045682.g001-2.png"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Milnesium_tardigradum","sci":"Milnesium tardigradum","status":null,"mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":11},
{"name":"Platypus","page":"Platypus","group":"Mammal","where":"Rivers and streams of eastern Australia","diet":"Insect larvae, shrimp, worms","facts":["It's a mammal that lays eggs.","Males have a venomous spur on their back legs.","It hunts with its eyes, ears and nose shut, sensing prey's electric fields with its bill."],"lesson":"Being one of a kind is fine. The platypus fits no category, and it has thrived for millions of years exactly as it is.","about":"The platypus, sometimes referred to as the duck-billed platypus, is a semiaquatic, egg-laying mammal endemic to eastern Australia, including Tasmania. The platypus is the sole living representative of the family Ornithorhynchidae and genus Ornithorhynchus, though a number of related species appear in the fossil record.","description":"Species of mammal","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1a/Duck-billed_platypus_%28Ornithorhynchus_anatinus%29_Scottsdale.jpg/1920px-Duck-billed_platypus_%28Ornithorhynchus_anatinus%29_Scottsdale.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Charles J. Sharp","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Duck-billed_platypus_(Ornithorhynchus_anatinus)_Scottsdale.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Platypus","sci":"Ornithorhynchus anatinus","status":"NT","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":12},
{"name":"Monarch Butterfly","page":"Monarch_butterfly","group":"Insect","where":"North America, migrating to Mexico","diet":"Milkweed (caterpillars), flower nectar (adults)","facts":["It migrates up to 4,800 km (3,000 miles) to forests in Mexico it has never seen.","The trip takes several generations; the great-grandchildren find the way back.","Milkweed makes it taste bad, and its orange warns birds."],"lesson":"Some journeys are bigger than one life. No single monarch makes the whole trip; each generation carries it forward for the next.","about":"The monarch butterfly or simply monarch is a milkweed butterfly in the family Nymphalidae. Other common names, depending on region, include milkweed, common tiger, wanderer, and black-veined brown. It is among the most familiar of North American butterflies and an iconic pollinator, although it is not an especially effective pollinator of milkweeds.","description":"Milkweed butterfly in the family Nymphalidae","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/73/Monarch_Butterfly_Danaus_plexippus_Male_2664px.jpg/1920px-Monarch_Butterfly_Danaus_plexippus_Male_2664px.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Photo by and (c)2007 Derek Ramsey (Ram-Man)","license":"GFDL 1.2","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Monarch_Butterfly_Danaus_plexippus_Male_2664px.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Monarch_butterfly","sci":"Danaus plexippus","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":13},
{"name":"Bald Eagle","page":"Bald_eagle","group":"Bird","where":"Across North America, near water","diet":"Fish, waterbirds, carrion","facts":["Its nests are the biggest of any North American bird: one weighed about 2 tonnes.","It isn't bald: \"bald\" used to mean white-headed.","It came back from near extinction after DDT was banned in 1972."],"lesson":"Build things that last. Eagles add to the same nest year after year until it weighs as much as a car: small efforts add up.","about":"The bald eagle is a bird of prey found in North America. A sea eagle, it has two known subspecies and forms a species pair with the white-tailed eagle, which occupies the same niche as the bald eagle in the Palearctic. Its range includes most of Canada and Alaska, all of the contiguous United States, and northern Mexico.","description":"Bird of prey species of North America","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/db/Bald_eagle_about_to_fly_in_Alaska_%282016%29.jpg/1920px-Bald_eagle_about_to_fly_in_Alaska_%282016%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Andy Morffew from Itchen Abbas, Hampshire, UK","license":"CC BY 2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bald_eagle_about_to_fly_in_Alaska_(2016).jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bald_eagle","sci":"Haliaeetus leucocephalus","status":"LC","mass":5.35,"length":null,"life":null,"n":14},
{"name":"Blue Whale","page":"Blue_whale","group":"Mammal","where":"Every ocean","diet":"Krill, up to about 4 tonnes a day","facts":["It's the largest animal that has ever lived, bigger than any dinosaur.","Its heart is about the size of a small car.","Its calls can travel hundreds of kilometers underwater."],"lesson":"The biggest things are built from the smallest. The largest animal ever lives on tiny krill, eaten one gulp at a time.","about":"The blue whale is a species of baleen whale and the largest marine mammal in the rorqual family Balaenopteridae. Reaching a maximum confirmed length of 29.9–30.5 m (98–100 ft) and weighing up to 190–200 t, it is the largest animal known to have ever existed. The blue whale's long and slender body can be of various shades of greyish-blue on its upper surface and somewhat lighter underneath.","description":"Species of whale; largest animal known","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1c/Anim1754_-_Flickr_-_NOAA_Photo_Library.jpg/1920px-Anim1754_-_Flickr_-_NOAA_Photo_Library.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"NOAA Photo Library","license":"Public domain","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Anim1754_-_Flickr_-_NOAA_Photo_Library.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue_whale","sci":"Balaenoptera musculus","status":"EN","mass":null,"length":27.1,"life":null,"n":15},
{"name":"Capybara","page":"Capybara","group":"Mammal","where":"Wetlands and rivers of South America","diet":"Grasses and water plants","facts":["It's the largest rodent in the world, up to about 65 kg (140 lb).","Other animals, from birds to monkeys, often sit on it.","Its eyes, ears and nostrils sit high on its head so it can hide underwater."],"lesson":"Calm is contagious. Animals of every kind relax around the capybara, because it doesn't make anything harder than it is.","about":"The capybara or greater capybara is the largest living rodent, native to all countries in South America except Chile. It is a semiaquatic herbivore that inhabits savannas and dense forests, living near and in bodies of freshwater and feeding mainly on grasses and aquatic plants.","description":"Largest species of rodents","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/71/Capybaracropped.jpg/1920px-Capybaracropped.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Giles Laurent","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Capybaracropped.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Capybara","sci":"Hydrochoerus hydrochaeris","status":"LC","mass":48.9,"length":null,"life":null,"n":16},
{"name":"Emperor Penguin","page":"Emperor_penguin","group":"Bird","where":"Antarctica","diet":"Fish, krill, squid","facts":["It's the tallest and heaviest penguin, about 1.2 meters (4 feet) tall.","The fathers keep the egg warm on their feet for about two months in winter, without eating.","Thousands huddle together and take turns on the cold outside edge."],"lesson":"We survive the cold together. Penguins take turns at the freezing edge of the huddle, so nobody has to carry the worst alone.","about":"The emperor penguin is the tallest and heaviest of all living penguin species and is endemic to Antarctica. The male and female are similar in plumage and size, reaching 100 cm (39 in) in length and weighing from 22 to 45 kg. Feathers of the head and back are black and sharply delineated from the white belly, pale-yellow breast and bright-yellow ear patches.","description":"Large flightless seabird endemic to Antarctica","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a3/Aptenodytes_forsteri_-Snow_Hill_Island%2C_Antarctica_-adults_and_juvenile-8.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"Ian Duffy from UK","license":"CC BY 2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aptenodytes_forsteri_-Snow_Hill_Island,_Antarctica_-adults_and_juvenile-8.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Emperor_penguin","sci":"Aptenodytes forsteri","status":"NT","mass":0.425,"length":null,"life":null,"n":17},
{"name":"Narwhal","page":"Narwhal","group":"Mammal","where":"Arctic waters around Greenland, Canada and Russia","diet":"Fish, squid, shrimp","facts":["Its \"horn\" is a tooth that can grow over 2.5 meters (8 feet) long.","The tusk has millions of nerve endings and may sense the water around it.","It can dive about 1,500 meters (5,000 feet) deep."],"lesson":"What makes you different can be your strength. The narwhal's odd tusk is a sense organ, not just a strange tooth.","about":"The narwhal is a species of toothed whale native to the Arctic. It is the only member of the genus Monodon and one of two living representatives of the family Monodontidae. The narwhal is a stocky cetacean with a relatively blunt snout, a large melon, and a shallow ridge in place of a dorsal fin.","description":"Medium-sized toothed whale species","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/bc/%D0%9D%D0%B0%D1%80%D0%B2%D0%B0%D0%BB_%D0%B2_%D1%80%D0%BE%D1%81%D1%81%D0%B8%D0%B9%D1%81%D0%BA%D0%BE%D0%B9_%D0%90%D1%80%D0%BA%D1%82%D0%B8%D0%BA%D0%B5.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"пресс-служба ПАО \"Газпром нефть\"","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D0%9D%D0%B0%D1%80%D0%B2%D0%B0%D0%BB_%D0%B2_%D1%80%D0%BE%D1%81%D1%81%D0%B8%D0%B9%D1%81%D0%BA%D0%BE%D0%B9_%D0%90%D1%80%D0%BA%D1%82%D0%B8%D0%BA%D0%B5.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Narwhal","sci":"Monodon monoceros","status":"LC","mass":80.0,"length":null,"life":null,"n":18},
{"name":"Ruby-throated Hummingbird","page":"Ruby-throated_hummingbird","group":"Bird","where":"Eastern North America in summer; Mexico, Central America and South Florida in winter","diet":"Flower nectar and tiny insects","facts":["It's the only hummingbird that regularly nests in the eastern United States.","Many fly nonstop across the Gulf of Mexico, about 800 km (500 miles), on migration.","Its heart can beat over 1,200 times a minute."],"lesson":"Small things can be fierce. Hummingbirds defend their flowers against birds many times their size, with energy that never quits.","about":"The ruby-throated hummingbird is a species of hummingbird that generally spends the winter in Central America, Mexico, and Florida, and migrates to Canada and other parts of Eastern North America for the summer to breed. It is the most common hummingbird in eastern North America, having population estimates of about 35 million in 2021.","description":"Species of bird","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/7c/Archilochus_colubris_-flying_-male-8.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"jeffreyw","license":"CC BY 2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Archilochus_colubris_-flying_-male-8.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ruby-throated_hummingbird","sci":"Archilochus colubris","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":19},
{"name":"Saltwater Crocodile","page":"Saltwater_crocodile","group":"Reptile","where":"Coasts and rivers from India to northern Australia","diet":"Fish, birds, mammals, almost anything","facts":["It's the largest living reptile, up to about 6 meters (20 feet).","It has the strongest bite ever measured in a living animal.","It can swim far out to sea, riding ocean currents between islands."],"lesson":"Stillness is a skill. Crocodiles spend most of their time waiting, and only move when it counts.","about":"The saltwater crocodile is a crocodilian native to saltwater habitats, brackish wetlands and freshwater rivers from India's east coast across Southeast Asia and the Sundaland to northern Australia and Micronesia. It has been listed as Least Concern on the IUCN Red List since 1996. It was hunted for its skin throughout its range up to the 1970s, and is threatened by illegal killing and habitat loss.","description":"Species of reptile","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/43/SaltwaterCrocodile%28%27Maximo%27%29.jpg/1920px-SaltwaterCrocodile%28%27Maximo%27%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Obtained from Molly Ebersold of the St. Augustine Alligator Farm","license":"Public domain","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:SaltwaterCrocodile(%27Maximo%27).jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saltwater_crocodile","sci":"Crocodylus porosus","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":20},
{"name":"Fennec Fox","page":"Fennec_fox","group":"Mammal","where":"Sahara Desert and North Africa","diet":"Insects, small rodents, plants","facts":["It's the smallest fox, with the biggest ears for its size.","Its ears help it hear prey underground and release heat.","Fur on the soles of its feet protects them from hot sand."],"lesson":"Turn hardship into an advantage. In the hottest desert, the fennec's huge ears both keep it cool and help it find food.","about":"The fennec fox is a small fox native to the deserts of North Africa, ranging from Western Sahara and Mauritania to the Sinai Peninsula. Its most distinctive feature is its unusually large ears, which serve to dissipate heat and listen for underground prey. The fennec is the smallest fox species.","description":"Species of fox","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9f/Fennec_Fox_Vulpes_zerda.jpg/1920px-Fennec_Fox_Vulpes_zerda.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Drew Avery","license":"CC BY 2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fennec_Fox_Vulpes_zerda.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fennec_fox","sci":"Vulpes zerda","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":21},
{"name":"Panther Chameleon","page":"Panther_chameleon","group":"Reptile","where":"Northern and eastern Madagascar","diet":"Insects","facts":["Its tongue can shoot out more than its body length in a fraction of a second.","Its eyes move separately, so it can look two ways at once.","Males from different parts of Madagascar wear different colors: red, orange, green or blue."],"lesson":"Your colors can say how you feel. Chameleons change color mostly to communicate: honesty about your state helps everyone around you.","about":"The panther chameleon is a species of chameleon found in the eastern and northern parts of Madagascar in a tropical forest biome. Additionally, it has been introduced to Réunion and Mauritius, as well as the state of Florida within the United States.","description":"Species of lizard","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2d/Panther_chameleon_%28Furcifer_pardalis%29_male_Nosy_Be.jpg/1920px-Panther_chameleon_%28Furcifer_pardalis%29_male_Nosy_Be.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Charles J. Sharp","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Panther_chameleon_(Furcifer_pardalis)_male_Nosy_Be.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Panther_chameleon","sci":"Furcifer pardalis","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":22},
{"name":"Western Honey Bee","page":"Western_honey_bee","group":"Insect","where":"Worldwide, with beekeepers","diet":"Flower nectar and pollen","facts":["It tells other bees where flowers are with a \"waggle dance.\"","A worker makes about a twelfth of a teaspoon of honey in her whole life.","A hive keeps its center at about 35 °C (95 °F) all year."],"lesson":"Small work matters. One bee makes a twelfth of a teaspoon of honey in its life, and together they feed a hive through winter.","about":"The western honey bee is the most common of the 7–12 species of honey bees worldwide. The genus name Apis is Latin for 'bee', and mellifera is the Latin for 'honey-bearing' or 'honey-carrying', referring to the species' production of honey.","description":"Species of honey bee","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4d/Apis_mellifera_Western_honey_bee.jpg/1920px-Apis_mellifera_Western_honey_bee.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Andreas Trepte","license":"CC BY-SA 2.5","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Apis_mellifera_Western_honey_bee.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Western_honey_bee","sci":"Apis mellifera","status":"DD","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":23},
{"name":"Great White Shark","page":"Great_white_shark","group":"Fish","where":"Cool coastal seas worldwide","diet":"Seals, fish, dolphins","facts":["It can sense a drop of blood in about 100 liters of water.","It may go through tens of thousands of teeth in its lifetime.","It can live for over 70 years."],"lesson":"Be curious, not just fearsome. White sharks often investigate with a careful bite, then let go: they test before they commit.","about":"The great white shark, also known as the white shark, white pointer, or great white, is a large shark. It is closely related to the mako sharks, the porbeagle, and the salmon shark. It is a robustly built species with a grayish upperside and a white underside.","description":"Species of large lamniform shark","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/56/White_shark.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"Pterantula (Terry Goss) at en.wikipedia","license":"CC BY 2.5","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:White_shark.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Great_white_shark","sci":"Carcharodon carcharias","status":"VU","mass":1088.621,"length":null,"life":73.0,"n":24},
{"name":"Masai Giraffe","page":"Masai_giraffe","group":"Mammal","where":"Savannas of Kenya and Tanzania","diet":"Leaves, especially acacia","facts":["It has the same number of neck bones as you: seven.","Its tongue is about 50 cm (20 in) long and dark, maybe to prevent sunburn.","A newborn drops about 1.5 meters (5 feet) to the ground at birth, and stands within an hour."],"lesson":"Rise above and see further. The giraffe's height lets it spot danger early and reach what others can't.","about":"The Masai giraffe, also spelled Maasai giraffe, and sometimes called the Kilimanjaro giraffe, is a species of giraffe. It is native to East Africa. The Masai giraffe can be found in central and southern Kenya, Tanzania, Uganda, and the Luangwa Valley region of Zambia.","description":"Species of giraffe","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/64/Masai_giraffes_%28Giraffa_tippelskirchi%29_Arusha.jpg/1920px-Masai_giraffes_%28Giraffa_tippelskirchi%29_Arusha.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Charles J. Sharp","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Masai_giraffes_(Giraffa_tippelskirchi)_Arusha.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Masai_giraffe","sci":"Giraffa tippelskirchi","status":null,"mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":25},
{"name":"Common Raccoon","page":"Raccoon","group":"Mammal","where":"North America, and now Europe and Japan","diet":"Almost anything: fruit, nuts, insects, trash","facts":["Its front paws are so sensitive it \"sees\" with them, especially when wet.","It can remember how to solve a puzzle for up to three years.","Its black mask may cut glare and help it see at night."],"lesson":"Use your hands and your mind. Raccoons solve problems by touch and memory: curiosity, kept up, becomes skill.","about":"The raccoon, sometimes called the North American, northern or common raccoon to distinguish it from other species of raccoon, is a mammal native to North America. It is the largest of the procyonid family, having a body length of 40 to 70 cm, and a body weight of 5 to 26 kg. Its grayish coat mostly consists of dense underfur, which insulates it against cold weather.","description":"Medium-sized mammal native to North America","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3e/Raccoon_in_Central_Park_%2835264%29.jpg/1920px-Raccoon_in_Central_Park_%2835264%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Rhododendrites","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Raccoon_in_Central_Park_(35264).jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Raccoon","sci":"Procyon lotor","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":20.0,"n":26},
{"name":"Sea Otter","page":"Sea_otter","group":"Mammal","where":"Coasts of the North Pacific","diet":"Sea urchins, clams, crabs","facts":["It holds hands with others while sleeping so it doesn't drift away.","It has the thickest fur of any animal: about a million hairs per square inch.","It uses rocks as tools to crack open shells on its belly."],"lesson":"Hold on to the ones you love. Sea otters hold hands while they sleep so the current never pulls them apart.","about":"The sea otter is a marine mammal native to the coasts of the northern and eastern North Pacific Ocean. Adult sea otters typically weigh between 14 and 45 kg (30–100 lb), making them the heaviest members of the weasel family, but among the smallest marine mammals. Unlike most marine mammals, the sea otter's primary form of insulation is an exceptionally thick coat of fur, the densest in the animal kingdom.","description":"Species of marine mammal","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/02/Sea_Otter_%28Enhydra_lutris%29_%2825169790524%29_crop.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"Marshal Hedin from San Diego","license":"CC BY-SA 2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sea_Otter_(Enhydra_lutris)_(25169790524)_crop.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sea_otter","sci":"Enhydra lutris","status":"EN","mass":45.0,"length":1.48,"life":null,"n":27},
{"name":"African Bush Elephant","page":"African_bush_elephant","group":"Mammal","where":"Savannas, bush and forests of Africa south of the Sahara","diet":"Grass, leaves, bark, fruit","facts":["It's the largest land animal.","Its trunk has around 40,000 muscles.","It can hear rumbles from other elephants kilometers away through its feet."],"lesson":"Remember, and grieve, and care. Elephants mourn their dead and protect their young as a family: deep bonds are a strength.","about":"The African bush elephant, also known as the African savanna elephant, is a species of elephant native to sub-Saharan Africa and the largest living terrestrial animal, with fully grown bulls reaching an average shoulder height of 3.04–3.36 m (10.0–11.0 ft) and a body mass of 5.2–6.9 t ; the largest recorded specimen had a shoulder height of 3.96 m (13.0 ft) and an estimated body mass of 10.4 t.","description":"Species of mammal","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/94/178_Male_African_bush_elephant_in_Etosha_National_Park_Photo_by_Giles_Laurent.jpg/1920px-178_Male_African_bush_elephant_in_Etosha_National_Park_Photo_by_Giles_Laurent.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Giles Laurent","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:178_Male_African_bush_elephant_in_Etosha_National_Park_Photo_by_Giles_Laurent.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/African_bush_elephant","sci":"Loxodonta africana","status":"EN","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":28},
{"name":"Cheetah","page":"Cheetah","group":"Mammal","where":"Savannas of Africa, and a few in Iran","diet":"Gazelles, impalas, hares","facts":["It's the fastest land animal: about 100 km/h (60 mph) in short bursts.","It goes from 0 to 100 km/h in about three seconds.","It can't roar, but it purrs."],"lesson":"Know your strengths and use them wisely. The cheetah's speed lasts only seconds, so it plans the chase before it starts.","about":"The cheetah is a species of large feline and the only living member of the genus Acinonyx. The cheetah is a pursuit predator and the fastest land animal, capable of running at 93–104 km/h (58–65 mph); it has evolved for speed, having powerful hindlimb muscles and a long lumbar spine. The cheetah has a tawny to creamy white or pale buff fur that is marked with evenly spaced, solid black spots.","description":"Large feline of the genus Acinonyx","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/92/Male_cheetah_facing_left_in_South_Africa.jpg/1920px-Male_cheetah_facing_left_in_South_Africa.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"AfricanConservation","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Male_cheetah_facing_left_in_South_Africa.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cheetah","sci":"Acinonyx jubatus","status":"VU","mass":40.2,"length":null,"life":null,"n":29},
{"name":"American Barn Owl","page":"American_barn_owl","group":"Bird","where":"The Americas, from southern Canada to the tip of South America","diet":"Mice, voles, shrews","facts":["It can catch prey in total darkness by sound alone.","One ear sits higher than the other, to pinpoint sounds up and down.","Soft-edged feathers make its flight almost silent."],"lesson":"Listen more than you look. The barn owl hunts in total darkness by sound alone.","about":"The American barn owl is usually considered a subspecies group and together with the western barn owl group, the eastern barn owl group, and sometimes the Andaman masked owl, make up the barn owl, cosmopolitan in range. The barn owl is recognized by most taxonomic authorities. A few separate them into distinct species, as is done here.","description":"Species of owl","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e8/American_Barn_Owl%2C_Bear_River%2C_Utah_%289637780911%29.jpg/1920px-American_Barn_Owl%2C_Bear_River%2C_Utah_%289637780911%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"USFWS Mountain-Prairie","license":"Public domain","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:American_Barn_Owl,_Bear_River,_Utah_(9637780911).jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/American_barn_owl","sci":"Tyto furcata","status":null,"mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":30},
{"name":"Strawberry Poison Frog","page":"Strawberry_poison-dart_frog","group":"Amphibian","where":"Rainforests of Nicaragua, Costa Rica and Panama","diet":"Ants and mites","facts":["Its bright red warns: \"don't eat me.\"","Its poison comes from the ants and mites it eats; frogs raised on other food aren't poisonous.","The mother carries her tadpoles one by one to tiny pools of water in plants, then feeds each one her own unfertilized eggs."],"lesson":"Bright can mean bold. The frog's colors are an honest warning, not a disguise: telling the truth about your boundaries keeps you safe.","about":"The strawberry poison frog, strawberry poison dart frog or blue jeans poison frog is a species of small poison dart frog found in Central America. It is common throughout its range, which extends from eastern central Nicaragua through Costa Rica and northwestern Panama. The species is often found in humid lowlands and premontane forest, but large populations are also found in disturbed areas such as plantations.","description":"Species of amphibian","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/35/Strawberry_poison_dart_frog_%2870539%29.jpg/1920px-Strawberry_poison_dart_frog_%2870539%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Rhododendrites","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Strawberry_poison_dart_frog_(70539).jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Strawberry_poison_dart_frog","sci":"Oophaga pumilio","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":31},
{"name":"Red Kangaroo","page":"Red_kangaroo","group":"Mammal","where":"Dry open lands of Australia","diet":"Grasses and shrubs","facts":["It can't walk backward easily.","It can hop about 9 meters (30 feet) in one leap.","A newborn is about the size of a jelly bean and crawls into the pouch."],"lesson":"You can't go backward easily, so keep moving forward. The kangaroo's body is built to go ahead, in big bounds.","about":"The red kangaroo is the largest of all kangaroos, the largest terrestrial mammal native to Australia, and the largest extant marsupial. It is found across mainland Australia, except for the more fertile areas, such as southern Western Australia, the eastern and southeastern coasts, and the rainforests along the northern coast.","description":"Species of mammal","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f1/Red_kangaroo_-_melbourne_zoo.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"fir0002 flagstaffotos [at] gmail.com Canon 20D + Canon 70-200mm f/2.8 L","license":"GFDL 1.2","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Red_kangaroo_-_melbourne_zoo.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Red_kangaroo","sci":"Osphranter rufus","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":32},
{"name":"Common Eastern Firefly","page":"Photinus_pyralis","group":"Insect","where":"Lawns, fields and forest edges of the eastern United States","diet":"Larvae eat snails, slugs and worms; adults barely eat","facts":["Its light is almost 100% efficient: nearly no heat.","Males flash while swooping upward in a J shape, which is why it's called the \"big dipper firefly.\"","A female answers from the grass with a flash of her own, timed just right, so the male can find her."],"lesson":"Shine without burning out. A firefly's light is almost all light and no heat: real brightness doesn't have to cost you everything.","about":"Photinus pyralis, also known by the common names the common eastern firefly or big dipper firefly, and sometimes called a \"lightning bug\", is a species of flying beetle. An organ on its abdomen is responsible for its light production. It is the most common species of firefly in North America and is typically found east of the Rocky Mountains.","description":"Species of beetle","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/9a/Photinus_pyralis_Firefly_3.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"art farmer from evansville indiana, usa","license":"CC BY-SA 2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Photinus_pyralis_Firefly_3.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Photinus_pyralis","sci":"Photinus pyralis","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":33},
{"name":"Orca","page":"Orca","group":"Mammal","where":"Every ocean, from the poles to the tropics","diet":"Fish, seals, sharks, even whales","facts":["It's the largest dolphin, not a whale.","Each family group has its own dialect of calls.","Grandmothers lead the pod and help their grandchildren survive."],"lesson":"Family and tradition carry wisdom. Orca pods have their own dialects, and grandmothers lead because experience matters.","about":"The orca, or killer whale, is a toothed whale and the largest member of the oceanic dolphin family. The only extant species in the genus Orcinus, it is recognizable by its distinct pigmentation; being mostly black on top, white on the bottom and having recognizable white eye patches.","description":"Largest living species of dolphin","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/37/Killerwhales_jumping.jpg/1920px-Killerwhales_jumping.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Robert Pittman","license":"Public domain","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Killerwhales_jumping.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orca","sci":"Orcinus orca","status":"DD","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":34},
{"name":"Galápagos Tortoise","page":"Galápagos_tortoise","group":"Reptile","where":"The Galápagos Islands","diet":"Grasses, leaves, cactus","facts":["It can live over 100 years.","It can go a year without food or water.","Each island's tortoises have their own shell shape."],"lesson":"Slow and steady really can last a century. The tortoise survives by conserving, enduring, and not rushing.","about":"The Galápagos tortoise, also called the Galápagos giant tortoise, is a very large species of tortoise in the genus Chelonoidis. The species comprises 15 subspecies. It is the largest living species of tortoise, and can weigh up to 417 kg (919 lb).","description":"Species of reptile","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/42/Galapagos_giant_tortoise_Geochelone_elephantopus.jpg/1920px-Galapagos_giant_tortoise_Geochelone_elephantopus.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Mfield, Matthew Field, http://www.photography.mattfield.com","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Galapagos_giant_tortoise_Geochelone_elephantopus.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gal%C3%A1pagos_tortoise","sci":null,"status":null,"mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":35},
{"name":"Great Hammerhead","page":"Great_hammerhead","group":"Fish","where":"Warm coastal seas worldwide","diet":"Stingrays, fish, squid","facts":["Its hammer-shaped head spreads out its sensors, like a metal detector.","It pins stingrays to the seafloor with its head.","It has nearly 360-degree vision up and down."],"lesson":"See the whole picture. The hammerhead's wide head gives it a nearly complete view of the world around it.","about":"The great hammerhead is the largest species of hammerhead shark, belonging to the family Sphyrnidae, attaining an average length of 4.6 m (15 ft) and reaching a maximum length of 6.2 m (20 ft). It is found around the globe in all the warmer parts of the oceans.","description":"Species of shark","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/bd/Great_hammerhead2.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"Albert kok","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Great_hammerhead2.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Great_hammerhead","sci":"Sphyrna mokarran","status":"CR","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":36},
{"name":"Snowy Owl","page":"Snowy_owl","group":"Bird","where":"The Arctic tundra","diet":"Lemmings, other small mammals, birds","facts":["Unlike most owls, it hunts in daylight: the Arctic summer has no night.","Males get whiter as they age.","One can eat over 1,600 lemmings in a year."],"lesson":"Adapt to your seasons. In the Arctic summer with no night, the snowy owl simply hunts by day.","about":"The snowy owl, also known as the polar owl, the white owl and the Arctic owl, is a large, white owl of the true owl family. Snowy owls are native to the Arctic regions of both North America and the Palearctic, breeding mostly on the tundra. It has a number of unique adaptations to its habitat and lifestyle, which are quite distinct from other extant owls.","description":"Species of owl","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f1/SnowyOwlAmericanBlackDuck.jpg/1920px-SnowyOwlAmericanBlackDuck.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Chuck Homler d/b/a Focus On Wildlife","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:SnowyOwlAmericanBlackDuck.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Snowy_owl","sci":"Bubo scandiacus","status":"VU","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":37},
{"name":"Nine-banded Armadillo","page":"Nine-banded_armadillo","group":"Mammal","where":"The Americas, including Florida","diet":"Insects, grubs, small animals","facts":["It almost always has identical quadruplets.","It can hold its breath for six minutes and walk across a stream bottom.","It jumps straight up when startled."],"lesson":"A little protection goes a long way. The armadillo's shell lets it keep exploring a world full of dangers.","about":"The nine-banded armadillo, also called the nine-banded long-nosed armadillo or common armadillo, is a species of armadillo native to South America. The Mexican long-nosed armadillo of North America was formerly treated as a subspecies of the nine-banded armadillo.","description":"Species of armadillo native to South America","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a7/Dasypus_novemcinctus_en_Zool%C3%B3gico_de_Paraguan%C3%A1.jpg/1920px-Dasypus_novemcinctus_en_Zool%C3%B3gico_de_Paraguan%C3%A1.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Aramburu Carlos","license":"CC BY 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dasypus_novemcinctus_en_Zool%C3%B3gico_de_Paraguan%C3%A1.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nine-banded_armadillo","sci":"Dasypus novemcinctus","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":38},
{"name":"Moon Jellyfish","page":"Moon_jellyfish","group":"Cnidarian","where":"Coastal seas worldwide","diet":"Plankton","facts":["It has no brain, heart or bones.","It's about 95% water.","Jellyfish have drifted in the oceans for over 500 million years, before dinosaurs."],"lesson":"Go with the flow, and endure. Jellyfish have drifted through the oceans for over 500 million years without a brain.","about":"Aurelia aurita is a species of true jellyfish in the family Ulmaridae and the type species of its genus, Aurelia. Before the 2020s, the species was considered cosmopolitan, but the taxon has since been split into at least 28 species. The species now identified as A. aurita is only found in the north Atlantic and in Argentina and is not dangerous to humans.","description":"Species of jellyfish","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f9/Aurelia_aurita_%28Cnidaria%29_Luc_Viatour.jpg/1920px-Aurelia_aurita_%28Cnidaria%29_Luc_Viatour.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Luc Viatour","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aurelia_aurita_(Cnidaria)_Luc_Viatour.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aurelia_aurita","sci":"Aurelia aurita","status":null,"mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":39},
{"name":"Little Brown Bat","page":"Little_brown_bat","group":"Mammal","where":"Across North America","diet":"Mosquitoes, moths, beetles","facts":["It finds food in the dark by echolocation: it shouts and listens for echoes.","It can eat hundreds of insects in an hour.","One wild little brown bat lived to 34 years old, remarkably long for so small an animal."],"lesson":"Find your way in the dark. Bats send out a call and listen for the echo: reach out, and the world answers back.","about":"The little brown bat or little brown myotis is an endangered species of mouse-eared microbat found in North America. It has a small body size and glossy brown fur. It is similar in appearance to several other mouse-eared bats, including the Indiana bat, northern long-eared bat, and Arizona myotis, to which it is closely related.","description":"Species of mammal found in North America","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/88/Little_Brown_Myotis_%28cropped%29.JPG/1920px-Little_Brown_Myotis_%28cropped%29.JPG?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"SMBishop","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Little_Brown_Myotis_(cropped).JPG"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Little_brown_bat","sci":"Myotis lucifugus","status":"EN","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":40},
{"name":"Arctic Fox","page":"Arctic_fox","group":"Mammal","where":"The Arctic tundra","diet":"Lemmings, birds, eggs, leftovers","facts":["Its coat turns white in winter and brown in summer.","It can handle cold down to about -50 °C (-58 °F).","It follows polar bears to eat their leftovers."],"lesson":"Change with the season. The Arctic fox turns white for winter and brown for summer, ready for whatever comes.","about":"The Arctic fox, also known as the white fox, polar fox, or snow fox, is a small species of fox native to the Arctic regions of the Northern Hemisphere and common throughout the Arctic tundra. It is well adapted to living in cold environments, and is known for its thick, warm fur that can be used as camouflage against snow in the winter. It has a large and fluffy tail.","description":"Species of fox","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dc/Vulpes_lagopus_in_Iceland_%28cropped_3%29.jpg/1920px-Vulpes_lagopus_in_Iceland_%28cropped_3%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Jonatan Pie (unsplash.com/@r3dmax)","license":"CC0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vulpes_lagopus_in_Iceland_(cropped_3).jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arctic_fox","sci":"Vulpes lagopus","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":41},
{"name":"Western Lowland Gorilla","page":"Western_lowland_gorilla","group":"Mammal","where":"Rainforests and swamps of central Africa","diet":"Leaves, stems, fruit","facts":["It's the most common gorilla, and the one you're most likely to see in a zoo.","Each gorilla's nose print is unique, like a fingerprint.","Koko, the gorilla who learned over 1,000 signs, was a western lowland gorilla."],"lesson":"True strength is gentle. Gorillas are enormously strong, yet they live calm, peaceful lives in close families.","about":"The western lowland gorilla is one of two critically endangered subspecies of the western gorilla that lives in montane, primary and secondary forest and lowland swampland in central Africa in Angola, Cameroon, Central African Republic, Republic of the Congo, Democratic Republic of the Congo, Equatorial Guinea and Gabon.","description":"Subspecies of ape","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/07/WesternLowlandGorilla03.jpg/1920px-WesternLowlandGorilla03.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Greg Hume","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:WesternLowlandGorilla03.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Western_lowland_gorilla","sci":"Gorilla gorilla gorilla","status":"CR","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":42},
{"name":"Lion","page":"Lion","group":"Mammal","where":"Savannas of Africa, and one forest in India","diet":"Zebras, wildebeest, buffalo","facts":["It's the only cat that lives in groups, called prides.","Its roar can be heard about 8 km (5 miles) away.","Lionesses do most of the hunting, as a team."],"lesson":"Lead as a team. Lionesses hunt together: no one has to do it all alone.","about":"The lion is a large cat of the genus Panthera, currently ranging only in Sub-Saharan Africa and India. It has a muscular, broad-chested body; a short, rounded head; round ears; and a dark, hairy tuft at the tip of its tail. It is sexually dimorphic; adult male lions are larger than females and have a prominent mane that extends from the head to the shoulders and chest.","description":"Large cat native to Africa and India","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a6/020_The_lion_king_Snyggve_in_the_Serengeti_National_Park_Photo_by_Giles_Laurent.jpg/3840px-020_The_lion_king_Snyggve_in_the_Serengeti_National_Park_Photo_by_Giles_Laurent.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"","license":"","page":""},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lion","sci":"Panthera leo","status":"VU","mass":188.0,"length":null,"life":null,"n":43},
{"name":"Common Bottlenose Dolphin","page":"Common_bottlenose_dolphin","group":"Mammal","where":"Warm and temperate seas worldwide","diet":"Fish and squid","facts":["Each dolphin has a signature whistle, like a name.","It sleeps with half of its brain at a time.","It uses sponges to protect its nose while foraging on the seafloor."],"lesson":"Know who you are. Each dolphin has a whistle that works like a name: identity helps us connect.","about":"The common bottlenose dolphin or Atlantic bottlenose dolphin is one of three species of bottlenose dolphin in the genus Tursiops. While formerly known simply as the bottlenose dolphin, this term is now applied to the genus Tursiops as a whole.","description":"Species of dolphin","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/bc/Tursiops_truncatus_01-cropped.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"NASA","license":"Public domain","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tursiops_truncatus_01-cropped.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Common_bottlenose_dolphin","sci":"Tursiops truncatus","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":44},
{"name":"Polar Bear","page":"Polar_bear","group":"Mammal","where":"The Arctic sea ice","diet":"Seals","facts":["Its fur is see-through and its skin is black.","It can smell a seal under a meter of snow.","It's counted as a marine mammal, because it lives on sea ice."],"lesson":"Patience feeds you. Polar bears wait beside breathing holes in the ice for hours, sometimes days.","about":"The polar bear is a large bear native to the Arctic and nearby areas. It is closely related to the brown bear, and the two species can interbreed. The polar bear is the largest extant species of bear and land carnivore by body mass, with adult males weighing 300–800 kg (660–1,760 lb).","description":"Species of bear native to the Arctic","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/66/Polar_Bear_-_Alaska_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"Alan Wilson","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Polar_Bear_-_Alaska_(cropped).jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Polar_bear","sci":"Ursus maritimus","status":"VU","mass":450.0,"length":null,"life":50.0,"n":45},
{"name":"American Flamingo","page":"American_flamingo","group":"Bird","where":"The Caribbean, the Galápagos and Florida","diet":"Shrimp, algae, small water animals","facts":["It's pink because of pigments in the food it eats.","It eats with its head upside down.","It often rests on one leg, which takes almost no effort."],"lesson":"You become what you take in. Flamingos are pink because of what they eat: choose what you feed your mind.","about":"The American flamingo is a large species of flamingo native to the West Indies, northern South America and the Yucatán Peninsula. It is closely related to the greater flamingo and Chilean flamingo, and was formerly considered conspecific with the greater flamingo, but that treatment is now widely viewed as incorrect due to a lack of evidence.","description":"Species of bird","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/21/American_Flamingo_JG.jpg/1920px-American_Flamingo_JG.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"JeffreyGammon","license":"CC BY 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:American_Flamingo_JG.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/American_flamingo","sci":"Phoenicopterus ruber","status":"LC","mass":3.579,"length":null,"life":null,"n":46},
{"name":"Common Ostrich","page":"Common_ostrich","group":"Bird","where":"Savannas and deserts of Africa","diet":"Plants, seeds, insects","facts":["It's the largest bird and lays the largest egg.","It can run about 70 km/h (43 mph).","Its eyes are bigger than its brain."],"lesson":"You don't have to fly to go far. The ostrich can't fly, but it can outrun almost anything.","about":"The common ostrich, or simply ostrich, is a species of flightless bird native to certain areas of Africa and is the largest living species of bird. The common ostrich is one of two extant species of ostriches, the only living members of the genus Struthio in the ratite group of birds.","description":"Species of flightless bird","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/9d/Struthio_camelus_-_Etosha_2014_%283%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"Yathin S Krishnappa","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Struthio_camelus_-_Etosha_2014_(3).jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Common_ostrich","sci":"Struthio camelus","status":"LC","mass":115.0,"length":null,"life":null,"n":47},
{"name":"Gray Wolf","page":"Wolf","group":"Mammal","where":"North America, Europe and Asia","diet":"Deer, elk, moose, smaller animals","facts":["A wolf's howl can carry about 10 km (6 miles) over open ground.","Every dog is descended from wolves.","Packs are usually families: a mother, a father and their pups."],"lesson":"We go further together. A wolf pack is a family that hunts, raises pups and travels as one.","about":"The wolf, also known as the grey wolf or gray wolf, is a canine native to Eurasia and North America. More than thirty subspecies of Canis lupus have been recognized, including the dog and dingo, though grey wolves, as popularly understood, include only naturally occurring wild subspecies.","description":"Species of canine","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/68/Eurasian_wolf_2.jpg/1920px-Eurasian_wolf_2.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"User:Mas3cf","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Eurasian_wolf_2.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wolf","sci":"Canis lupus","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":48},
{"name":"Brown-throated Sloth","page":"Brown-throated_sloth","group":"Mammal","where":"Rainforests of Central and South America","diet":"Leaves","facts":["It comes down from its tree about once a week, to go to the bathroom.","Algae grows in its fur, giving it a green tint.","It can hold its breath for up to 40 minutes."],"lesson":"Slowing down can be a strategy. The sloth survives by moving so little that predators barely notice it.","about":"The brown-throated sloth is a species of three-toed sloth found in the Neotropical realm of Central and South America.","description":"Species of New World mammal","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/18/Bradypus.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"Stefan Laube (Tauchgurke)","license":"Public domain","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bradypus.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brown-throated_sloth","sci":"Bradypus variegatus","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":49},
{"name":"Ocellaris Clownfish","page":"Ocellaris_clownfish","group":"Fish","where":"Coral reefs of the eastern Indian Ocean and the western Pacific","diet":"Algae, plankton, scraps from its anemone","facts":["It lives safely among stinging anemone tentacles, thanks to a slimy coat.","All are born male; the largest in a group becomes female.","It never strays more than a few meters from its anemone."],"lesson":"Find your safe place and make it home. The clownfish lives among stinging tentacles that protect it, because it learned to belong there.","about":"The ocellaris clownfish, also known as the false percula clownfish or common clownfish, is a marine fish belonging to the family Pomacentridae, which includes clownfishes and damselfishes. A. ocellaris are found in different colors, depending on where they are located. For example, black A. ocellaris with white bands can be found near northern Hawaii, USA, North America, Australia, Southeast Asia, and Japan.","description":"Species of fish","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f6/Clown_fish_in_the_Andaman_Coral_Reef.jpg/1920px-Clown_fish_in_the_Andaman_Coral_Reef.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Ritiks","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Clown_fish_in_the_Andaman_Coral_Reef.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ocellaris_clownfish","sci":"Amphiprion ocellaris","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":50},
{"name":"Lined Seahorse","page":"Lined_seahorse","group":"Fish","where":"Western Atlantic coasts, from Canada to Uruguay, including Florida","diet":"Tiny shrimp","facts":["The father gets pregnant and gives birth.","It moves each eye on its own.","It's one of the slowest swimmers of any fish."],"lesson":"Care isn't one person's job. Seahorse fathers carry the babies: sharing the load is how families thrive.","about":"The lined seahorse, northern seahorse or spotted seahorse, is a species of fish that belongs to the family Syngnathidae. H. erectus is a diurnal species with an approximate length of 15 cm and lifespan of one to four years. The H. erectus species can be found in myriad colors, from greys and blacks to reds, greens, and oranges.","description":"Species of fish","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/bf/Lined_Seahorse-_Hippocampus_erectus_%286042886100%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"MattSullivan from Boston, MA, USA","license":"CC BY 2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lined_Seahorse-_Hippocampus_erectus_(6042886100).jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lined_seahorse","sci":"Hippocampus erectus","status":"VU","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":51},
{"name":"Tiger","page":"Tiger","group":"Mammal","where":"Forests of South and Southeast Asia, and Russia's Far East","diet":"Deer, wild pigs, buffalo","facts":["It's the largest cat.","Its stripes are on its skin too, not just its fur.","Unlike most cats, it likes water and swims well."],"lesson":"Be comfortable alone. Tigers live solitary lives with confidence: solitude can be strength, not loneliness.","about":"The tiger is a large cat and a member of the genus Panthera native to Asia. It has a powerful, muscular body with a large head and paws, a long tail and orange fur that fades to white in parts, with black, mostly vertical stripes. It is traditionally classified into nine subspecies, though some recognise only two subspecies, the mainland Asian tiger and the Sunda Islands tiger.","description":"Species of large cat","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b0/Bengal_tiger_%28Panthera_tigris_tigris%29_female_3_crop.jpg/1920px-Bengal_tiger_%28Panthera_tigris_tigris%29_female_3_crop.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Charles J. Sharp","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bengal_tiger_(Panthera_tigris_tigris)_female_3_crop.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tiger","sci":"Panthera tigris","status":"EN","mass":180.0,"length":null,"life":null,"n":52},
{"name":"Koala","page":"Koala","group":"Mammal","where":"Eucalyptus forests of eastern Australia","diet":"Eucalyptus leaves","facts":["It sleeps up to 20 hours a day.","Its fingerprints are so like ours they could confuse a crime scene.","Eucalyptus is so low in energy and so toxic that few other animals can live on it."],"lesson":"Rest is part of the work. Koalas sleep most of the day so they have the energy their tough diet demands.","about":"The koala, sometimes inaccurately called the koala bear, is an arboreal herbivorous marsupial native to Australia. It is the only extant representative of the family Phascolarctidae. Its closest living relatives are the wombats.","description":"Arboreal herbivorous marsupial native to Australia","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/49/Koala_climbing_tree.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"Diliff","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Koala_climbing_tree.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Koala","sci":"Phascolarctos cinereus","status":"VU","mass":null,"length":null,"life":10.0,"n":53},
{"name":"Jaguar","page":"Jaguar","group":"Mammal","where":"Rainforests and wetlands from Mexico to Argentina","diet":"Capybaras, caimans, deer, turtles, fish","facts":["It has the strongest bite of any big cat for its size, strong enough to crack a turtle's shell.","Unlike most cats, it loves water and swims well.","It often kills with a bite straight through the skull."],"lesson":"Power is best used precisely. The jaguar doesn't waste its strength; it puts it exactly where it counts.","about":"The jaguar is a large cat species and the only living member of the genus Panthera that is native to the Americas. Its coat features pale yellow to tan colored fur covered with spots that transition to rosettes on the sides; some individuals have a melanistic spotted coat.","description":"Large cat native to the Americas","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/0a/Standing_jaguar.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"USFWS","license":"Public domain","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Standing_jaguar.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jaguar","sci":"Panthera onca","status":"NT","mass":0.8,"length":null,"life":null,"n":54},
{"name":"Ocelot","page":"Ocelot","group":"Mammal","where":"Forests and thick brush from southern Texas to northern Argentina","diet":"Rodents, rabbits, birds, lizards","facts":["No two ocelots have the same pattern of spots and stripes.","It hunts mostly at night and rests in trees by day.","A few dozen still live wild in southern Texas."],"lesson":"Your pattern is yours alone. No two ocelots look alike, and none of them tries to.","about":"The ocelot is a medium-sized spotted wild cat that reaches 40–50 cm (16–20 in) at the shoulders and weighs between 7 and 15.5 kg on average. It is native to the southwestern United States, Mexico, Central and South America, and the Caribbean islands of Trinidad and Margarita. Carl Linnaeus scientifically described it in 1758.","description":"Species of wild cat","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/016_Ocelot_in_Encontro_das_%C3%81guas_State_Park_Photo_by_Giles_Laurent.jpg/1920px-016_Ocelot_in_Encontro_das_%C3%81guas_State_Park_Photo_by_Giles_Laurent.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Giles Laurent","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:016_Ocelot_in_Encontro_das_%C3%81guas_State_Park_Photo_by_Giles_Laurent.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ocelot","sci":"Leopardus pardalis","status":"LC","mass":13.5,"length":null,"life":null,"n":55},
{"name":"Caracal","page":"Caracal","group":"Mammal","where":"Dry savannas and scrub of Africa, the Middle East and India","diet":"Birds, hares, rodents, small antelope","facts":["It can leap about 3 meters (10 feet) straight up to knock birds out of the air.","Its long black ear tufts may help it hear, and signal to other caracals.","Its name comes from the Turkish for \"black ear.\""],"lesson":"Reach higher than people expect. The caracal snatches birds out of the sky that never thought a cat could reach them.","about":"The caracal is a wild cat native to Africa, the Middle East, Central Asia, and arid areas of Pakistan and northwestern India. It is characterised by a robust build, long legs, a short face, long tufted ears, relatively short tail, and long canine teeth. Its coat is uniformly reddish tan or sandy, while the ventral parts are lighter with small reddish markings.","description":"Medium-sized wild cat","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/75/Caracal_on_the_road%2C_early_morning_in_Kgalagadi_%2836173878220%29_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"Derek Keats","license":"CC BY 2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Caracal_on_the_road,_early_morning_in_Kgalagadi_(36173878220)_(cropped).jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Caracal","sci":"Caracal caracal","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":56},
{"name":"Serval","page":"Serval","group":"Mammal","where":"Grasslands and wetlands of Africa south of the Sahara","diet":"Rodents, birds, frogs, insects","facts":["It has the longest legs for its size of any cat.","Its huge ears can hear rodents moving underground.","About half of its pounces end in a catch, one of the best rates of any cat."],"lesson":"Listen before you leap. The serval stands still and listens first, which is why so many of its pounces land.","about":"The serval is a wild small cat native to Africa. It is widespread in sub-Saharan countries, where it inhabits grasslands, wetlands, moorlands and bamboo thickets. Across its range, it occurs in protected areas, and hunting it is either prohibited or regulated in range countries.","description":"Medium-sized wild cat","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f6/Servalcropped.png/1920px-Servalcropped.png?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Diego Delso","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Servalcropped.png"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Serval","sci":"Leptailurus serval","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":57},
{"name":"Clouded Leopard","page":"Clouded_leopard","group":"Mammal","where":"Forests of Southeast Asia and the Himalayan foothills","diet":"Monkeys, deer, birds, wild pigs","facts":["For its size, it has the longest canine teeth of any living cat.","It can climb down trees head first and hang upside down by its back feet.","Its cloud-shaped markings make it hard to see in the shadows of the forest."],"lesson":"Flexibility is a kind of strength. The clouded leopard goes where other cats can't, by bending in ways they don't.","about":"The clouded leopard, also called mainland clouded leopard, is a wild cat that has large dusky-grey blotches and irregular spots and stripes reminiscent of clouds. Its head-and-body length ranges from 68.6 to 108 cm with a 61 to 91 cm long tail, which it uses for balancing when moving in trees.","description":"Species of wild cat","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/39/Neofelis_nebulosa%2C_Clouded_leopard.jpg/1920px-Neofelis_nebulosa%2C_Clouded_leopard.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Rushenb","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Neofelis_nebulosa,_Clouded_leopard.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Clouded_leopard","sci":"Neofelis nebulosa","status":"VU","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":58},
{"name":"Eurasian Lynx","page":"Eurasian_lynx","group":"Mammal","where":"Forests of Europe, Siberia and Central Asia","diet":"Roe deer, hares, rodents, birds","facts":["It's the largest lynx, up to about 30 kg (66 lb).","Its huge furry paws work like snowshoes.","It was brought back to forests in Switzerland, Germany and France after vanishing there."],"lesson":"Second chances can work. The lynx came back to forests that had lost it, once people made room for it again.","about":"The Eurasian lynx is one of the four extant species within the medium-sized wild cat genus Lynx. It is widely distributed from Northern, Central and Eastern Europe to Central Asia and Siberia, the Tibetan Plateau and the Himalayas. It inhabits temperate and boreal forests up to an elevation of 5,500 m (18,000 ft).","description":"Species of medium-sized cat native to Eurasia","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/49/Lynx_Nationalpark_Bayerischer_Wald_01.jpg/1920px-Lynx_Nationalpark_Bayerischer_Wald_01.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Aconcagua (talk)","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lynx_Nationalpark_Bayerischer_Wald_01.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eurasian_lynx","sci":"Lynx lynx","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":59},
{"name":"Bobcat","page":"Bobcat","group":"Mammal","where":"Most of North America, from southern Canada to Mexico, including Florida","diet":"Rabbits, rodents, birds","facts":["It's named for its short, \"bobbed\" tail.","It lives near people more often than they know, even in the suburbs.","It's about twice the size of a house cat."],"lesson":"You can thrive close to others without being seen. The bobcat shares the suburbs with millions of people, quietly.","about":"The bobcat, also known as the wildcat, bay lynx, or red lynx, is one of the four extant species within the medium-sized wild cat genus Lynx. Native to North America, it ranges from southern Canada through most of the contiguous United States to Oaxaca in Mexico. It is listed as Least Concern on the IUCN Red List since 2002, due to its wide distribution and large population.","description":"Medium-sized North American wild cat","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4e/Bobcat_at_Columbus_Zoo_Boo.jpg/1920px-Bobcat_at_Columbus_Zoo_Boo.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Becker1999 (Paul and Cathy)","license":"CC BY 2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bobcat_at_Columbus_Zoo_Boo.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bobcat","sci":"Lynx rufus","status":"LC","mass":0.31,"length":null,"life":null,"n":60},
{"name":"Cougar","page":"Cougar","group":"Mammal","where":"The Americas, from Canada to the tip of South America, including the Florida panther","diet":"Deer, elk, smaller mammals","facts":["It has more names than any other animal: puma, mountain lion, panther, catamount and dozens more.","It can't roar; it purrs, hisses and makes an eerie scream.","Florida's panthers are cougars, and only about 120 to 230 are left."],"lesson":"Be adaptable. The cougar lives from Canadian mountains to Florida swamps, more places than almost any other wild mammal in the Americas.","about":"The cougar, also called puma, mountain lion, catamount, and panther, is a large feline native to the Americas. It inhabits North, Central and South America, making it the most widely distributed wild, terrestrial mammal in the Western Hemisphere, and one of the most widespread in the world. Its range spans Yukon, British Columbia and Alberta in Canada, the Rocky Mountains and areas in the western United States.","description":"Wild cat species native to the Americas","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d6/Mountain_Lion_in_Glacier_National_Park.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"National Park Service","license":"Public domain","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mountain_Lion_in_Glacier_National_Park.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cougar","sci":"Puma concolor","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":61},
{"name":"Pallas's Cat","page":"Pallas's_cat","group":"Mammal","where":"Cold, rocky grasslands of Central Asia","diet":"Pikas, voles, small birds","facts":["It has the densest fur of any cat, for winters far below freezing.","Its round pupils (not slits) and flat face make it look permanently grumpy.","It's about the size of a house cat but looks bigger because of its fur."],"lesson":"Don't judge by the face. Pallas's cat looks grumpy, but it's just built for a hard place.","about":"Pallas's cat, also called manul, is a small wild cat with long and dense light grey fur, and rounded ears set low on the sides of the head. Its head-and-body length ranges from 46 to 65 cm with a 21–31 cm (8.3–12.2 in) long bushy tail. It is well camouflaged and adapted to the cold continental climate in its native range, which receives little rainfall and experiences a wide range of temperatures.","description":"Species of small wild cat","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d6/Manoel.jpg/1920px-Manoel.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Sander van der Wel","license":"CC BY-SA 2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Manoel.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pallas's_cat","sci":"Otocolobus manul","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":62},
{"name":"Sand Cat","page":"Sand_cat","group":"Mammal","where":"Sandy deserts of North Africa, Arabia and Central Asia","diet":"Rodents, birds, reptiles, insects","facts":["Thick fur on the soles of its feet protects them from hot sand and hides its tracks.","It can go weeks without drinking; it gets water from its food.","It digs its prey out of burrows with its paws."],"lesson":"Small and tough beats big and fragile. The sand cat thrives where almost nothing else can live.","about":"The sand cat is a small wild cat that inhabits sandy and stony deserts far from water sources. With its sandy to light grey fur, it is well camouflaged in a desert environment. Its head-and-body length ranges from 39–52 cm (15–20 in) with a 23–31 cm (9.1–12.2 in) long tail.","description":"Small wild cat species (Felis margarita)","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e9/Persian_sand_CAT.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"Payman sazesh","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Persian_sand_CAT.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sand_cat","sci":"Felis margarita","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":63},
{"name":"Fishing Cat","page":"Fishing_cat","group":"Mammal","where":"Wetlands and mangroves of South and Southeast Asia","diet":"Fish, frogs, crabs, birds","facts":["Its front toes are partly webbed, for swimming.","It taps the water's surface with its paw to lure fish, then scoops them out.","It even dives in after fish."],"lesson":"Use what's in front of you. The fishing cat made a living from water, which most cats avoid.","about":"The fishing cat is a medium-sized wild cat of South and Southeast Asia. It has a deep yellowish-grey fur with black lines and spots. Adults have a head-to-body length of 57 to 78 cm, with a 20 to 30 cm long tail.","description":"Small wild cat","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f7/Prionailurus_viverrinus_01.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"Kelinahandbasket","license":"CC BY 2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Prionailurus_viverrinus_01.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fishing_cat","sci":"Prionailurus viverrinus","status":"VU","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":64},
{"name":"Leopard","page":"Leopard","group":"Mammal","where":"Africa south of the Sahara and parts of Asia","diet":"Antelope, monkeys, wild pigs, almost anything","facts":["It can haul prey heavier than itself up into a tree.","Its spots are rosettes: rings around a lighter center.","It lives in more kinds of places than any other big cat: rainforest, desert, mountains, even the edges of cities."],"lesson":"Keep what you've earned safe. The leopard carries its meal into a tree, out of reach of lions and hyenas.","about":"The leopard is one of the five extant cat species in the genus Panthera. It has a pale yellowish to dark golden fur with dark spots grouped in rosettes. Its body is slender and muscular, reaching a length of 92–183 cm (36–72 in) with a 66–102 cm (26–40 in) long tail and a shoulder height of 60–70 cm (24–28 in).","description":"Species of cat native to Africa and Asia","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/70/African_leopard_male_%28cropped%29.jpg/1920px-African_leopard_male_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Sumeet Moghe","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:African_leopard_male_(cropped).jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leopard","sci":"Panthera pardus","status":"VU","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":65},
{"name":"Maned Wolf","page":"Maned_wolf","group":"Mammal","where":"Grasslands and savannas of central South America","diet":"Fruit (especially the \"wolf apple\"), rodents, birds","facts":["It isn't a wolf or a fox; it's the only species in its genus.","Its very long legs let it see over tall grass.","Its urine smells so strongly of skunk that it's called the \"skunk wolf.\""],"lesson":"It's fine to be your own thing. The maned wolf looks like a fox on stilts and fits no family but its own.","about":"The maned wolf is a large canine of South America. It is found in Argentina, Brazil, Bolivia, Peru, and Paraguay, and is almost locally extinct in Uruguay. Its markings resemble those of a red fox but it is neither a fox nor a wolf.","description":"Species of carnivore","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a3/Lobo_Guar%C3%A1_andando.jpg/1920px-Lobo_Guar%C3%A1_andando.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Jonathan Wilkins","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lobo_Guar%C3%A1_andando.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maned_wolf","sci":"Chrysocyon brachyurus","status":"NT","mass":23.0,"length":null,"life":null,"n":66},
{"name":"African Wild Dog","page":"African_wild_dog","group":"Mammal","where":"Savannas and woodlands of southern and eastern Africa","diet":"Impalas, gazelles, wildebeest calves","facts":["Its hunts succeed up to 80% of the time, among the best of any predator.","Pack members \"vote\" on when to go hunting by sneezing.","Each dog's patchy coat pattern is unique."],"lesson":"A team that shares beats a team of stars. Wild dogs feed their pups and their sick first, and they're among the most successful hunters on Earth.","about":"The African wild dog, also called painted dog and Cape hunting dog, is a wild canine native to sub-Saharan Africa. It is the largest wild canine in Africa and the only extant member of the genus Lycaon, which is distinguished from Canis by dentition highly specialised for a hypercarnivorous diet and by a lack of dewclaws.","description":"Species of canine","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/75/African_Wild_Dog_at_Working_with_Wildlife.jpg/1920px-African_Wild_Dog_at_Working_with_Wildlife.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"AfricanConservation","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:African_Wild_Dog_at_Working_with_Wildlife.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/African_wild_dog","sci":"Lycaon pictus","status":"EN","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":67},
{"name":"Dhole","page":"Dhole","group":"Mammal","where":"Forests of South and Southeast Asia","diet":"Deer, wild pigs","facts":["It whistles to keep its pack together in thick forest.","A pack can take down prey ten times the size of one dhole.","It's sometimes called the \"whistling dog.\""],"lesson":"Stay in touch. Dholes whistle to each other so the pack never loses anyone in the forest.","about":"The dhole is a canid native to South, East and Southeast Asia. The sole member of the genus Cuon, it is anatomically distinguished from members of the genus Canis in several aspects: its skull is convex rather than concave in profile, it lacks a third lower molar, and the upper molars possess only a single cusp as opposed to between two and four.","description":"Species of mammal","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/01/Dhole_%28Asiatic_wild_dog%29_cropped.jpg/1920px-Dhole_%28Asiatic_wild_dog%29_cropped.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Davidvraju","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dhole_(Asiatic_wild_dog)_cropped.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dhole","sci":"Cuon alpinus","status":"EN","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":68},
{"name":"Red Fox","page":"Red_fox","group":"Mammal","where":"Across the Northern Hemisphere and Australia","diet":"Rodents, rabbits, birds, fruit, scraps","facts":["It's the most widespread wild carnivore on Earth.","It may use the Earth's magnetic field to aim its pounce.","Its hearing is so sharp it can hear a mouse squeak about 40 meters (130 feet) away."],"lesson":"Curiosity takes you far. The red fox's willingness to try new places made it the most widespread carnivore in the world.","about":"The red fox is the largest of the true foxes, and the most widely distributed member of the order Carnivora. It is present across the entire Northern Hemisphere, including most of North America, Europe and Asia, as well as parts of North Africa.","description":"Species of mammal","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d2/Portrait_of_a_red_fox_in_Rautas_fj%C3%A4llurskog_%28cropped%29.jpg/1920px-Portrait_of_a_red_fox_in_Rautas_fj%C3%A4llurskog_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"ClaudiaTen","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_a_red_fox_in_Rautas_fj%C3%A4llurskog_(cropped).jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Red_fox","sci":"Vulpes vulpes","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":0.425,"life":5.0,"n":69},
{"name":"Bat-eared Fox","page":"Bat-eared_fox","group":"Mammal","where":"Dry grasslands of eastern and southern Africa","diet":"Termites, beetles, other insects","facts":["Its ears are about 13 cm (5 in) long and help it hear insects underground.","It has more teeth than almost any other mammal with a placenta: 46 to 50.","Termites make up most of its food."],"lesson":"Small things add up. The bat-eared fox lives almost entirely on termites, eaten by the thousand.","about":"The bat-eared fox is a species of fox found on the African savanna. It is the only extant species of the genus Otocyon, and is the sister species to all other members of the tribe Vulpini, not genetically being a true fox. Fossil records indicate this canid first appeared during the middle Pleistocene.","description":"Species of carnivorous mammal","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/84/Otocyon_megalotis_-_Etosha_2014.jpg/1920px-Otocyon_megalotis_-_Etosha_2014.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Yathin S Krishnappa","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Otocyon_megalotis_-_Etosha_2014.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bat-eared_fox","sci":"Otocyon megalotis","status":"LC","mass":4.15,"length":null,"life":null,"n":70},
{"name":"Ethiopian Wolf","page":"Ethiopian_wolf","group":"Mammal","where":"High mountain meadows of Ethiopia","diet":"Giant mole rats and other rodents","facts":["It's the rarest wild dog in Africa: fewer than 500 are left.","It hunts alone, but lives and patrols in a family pack.","It lives above about 3,000 meters (10,000 feet)."],"lesson":"You can be independent and still belong. Ethiopian wolves hunt alone but come home to their pack every day.","about":"The Ethiopian wolf, also called the red jackal, the Simien jackal or Simien fox, is a canine native to the Ethiopian Highlands, including the Simien Mountains. In southeastern Ethiopia, it is also known as the horse jackal. It is similar to the coyote in size and build, and is distinguished by its long and narrow skull, and its red and white fur.","description":"Canine native to Ethiopian Highlands","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e8/Ethiopian_wolf_%28Canis_simensis_citernii%29.jpg/1920px-Ethiopian_wolf_%28Canis_simensis_citernii%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Charles J. Sharp","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ethiopian_wolf_(Canis_simensis_citernii).jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ethiopian_wolf","sci":"Canis simensis","status":"EN","mass":19.0,"length":1.01,"life":null,"n":71},
{"name":"Coyote","page":"Coyote","group":"Mammal","where":"All of North America and Central America, including cities","diet":"Rodents, rabbits, fruit, insects, scraps","facts":["It has spread across North America as wolves disappeared, and now lives in nearly every big city.","It can run up to about 65 km/h (40 mph).","Its \"yip-howl\" chorus can make two coyotes sound like a dozen."],"lesson":"Adapt, don't retreat. Coyotes didn't shrink back as cities grew; they learned to live in them.","about":"The coyote is a species of canine also known as the American jackal, prairie wolf, and brush wolf. It is native to North America, and it is smaller than its close relative, the gray wolf, and slightly smaller than the closely related eastern wolf and red wolf. It fills much of the same ecological niche as the golden jackal does in Eurasia, but the coyote is generally larger.","description":"Species of canine native to North America","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/9c/2009-Coyote-Yosemite.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"Yathin S Krishnappa","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2009-Coyote-Yosemite.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coyote","sci":"Canis latrans","status":"LC","mass":0.263,"length":null,"life":null,"n":72},
{"name":"Gray Fox","page":"Gray_fox","group":"Mammal","where":"North and Central America, including Florida","diet":"Rodents, rabbits, fruit, insects","facts":["It's one of the only dogs that can climb trees, with hooked claws and rotating wrists.","It's one of the oldest kinds of dog alive, little changed in millions of years.","It often rests in trees by day."],"lesson":"Know your escape route. The gray fox climbs out of trouble instead of outrunning it.","about":"The gray fox, or grey fox, is an omnivorous mammal of the family Canidae, widespread throughout North America and Central America. This species and its only congener, the diminutive island fox of the California Channel Islands, are the only living members of the genus Urocyon, which is considered to be genetically sister to all other living canids. Its species name cinereoargenteus means \"ashen silver\".","description":"Species of canid","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a3/Gray_fox.jpg/1920px-Gray_fox.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"VJAnderson","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gray_fox.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gray_fox","sci":"Urocyon cinereoargenteus","status":"LC","mass":0.086,"length":null,"life":null,"n":73},
{"name":"Giant Panda","page":"Giant_panda","group":"Mammal","where":"Mountain bamboo forests of central China","diet":"Bamboo, about 99% of its diet","facts":["It eats for 10 to 16 hours a day: up to about 38 kg (84 lb) of bamboo.","It has a \"false thumb,\" a wrist bone that helps it grip bamboo.","It was moved from \"endangered\" to \"vulnerable\" in 2016, thanks to protection."],"lesson":"Protection works. The giant panda's numbers grew once its forests were saved.","about":"The giant panda, also known as the panda bear or simply panda, is a bear species endemic to China. It is characterised by its white coat with black patches around the eyes, ears, legs and shoulders. Its body is rotund; adult individuals weigh 100 to 115 kg and are typically 1.2 to 1.9 m long.","description":"Species of bear","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0f/Grosser_Panda.JPG/1920px-Grosser_Panda.JPG?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"J. Patrick Fischer","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Grosser_Panda.JPG"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Giant_panda","sci":"Ailuropoda melanoleuca","status":"VU","mass":117.5,"length":null,"life":null,"n":74},
{"name":"Sun Bear","page":"Sun_bear","group":"Mammal","where":"Tropical forests of Southeast Asia","diet":"Insects, honey, fruit","facts":["It's the smallest bear.","Its tongue can be about 25 cm (10 in) long, for licking honey and insects out of trees.","The gold patch on its chest is different on every bear."],"lesson":"Small can be mighty. The sun bear is the smallest bear, and still climbs higher and digs harder than most.","about":"The sun bear is a bear species in the family Ursidae found in the tropical forests of Southeast Asia. It is the only species in the genus Helarctos and the smallest bear species, standing nearly 70 cm (28 in) at the shoulder and weighing 25–65 kg (55–143 lb). It is stockily built, with large paws, strongly curved claws, small, rounded ears and a short snout.","description":"Tropical species of bear","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/eb/Sun-bear.jpg/1920px-Sun-bear.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Rushenb","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sun-bear.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sun_bear","sci":"Helarctos malayanus","status":"VU","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":75},
{"name":"Spectacled Bear","page":"Spectacled_bear","group":"Mammal","where":"Cloud forests of the Andes, from Venezuela to Bolivia","diet":"Bromeliads, fruit, cactus, sometimes meat","facts":["It's the only bear in South America.","The pale rings around its eyes look like glasses, and each bear's are different.","It builds platforms of branches in trees to rest and eat in."],"lesson":"Make yourself at home wherever you are. The spectacled bear builds its own resting spots high in the trees.","about":"The spectacled bear, also known as the Andean bear, is a species of bear native to the Andes Mountains in northern and western South America. It is the only living species of bear native to South America, and the last remaining short-faced bear. Unlike other omnivorous bears, the diet of the spectacled bear is mostly herbivorous.","description":"Species of mammal","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/56/Tremarctos_ornatus_in_Jardim_Zool%C3%B3gico_de_Curitiba.jpg/1920px-Tremarctos_ornatus_in_Jardim_Zool%C3%B3gico_de_Curitiba.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Wilfredor","license":"CC0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tremarctos_ornatus_in_Jardim_Zool%C3%B3gico_de_Curitiba.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spectacled_bear","sci":"Tremarctos ornatus","status":"VU","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":76},
{"name":"Grizzly Bear","page":"Grizzly_bear","group":"Mammal","where":"Mountains and forests of western North America","diet":"Roots, berries, salmon, moths, carrion","facts":["Its shoulder hump is pure muscle, for digging.","It can smell food from miles away.","It can eat around 20,000 calories a day before winter."],"lesson":"Prepare in the good times. Grizzlies eat all autumn so they can rest all winter.","about":"The grizzly bear, also known as the North American brown bear or simply grizzly, is a population or subspecies of the brown bear inhabiting North America.","description":"Subspecies of brown bear","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a9/GrizzlyBearJeanBeaufort.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"Jean Beaufort","license":"CC0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:GrizzlyBearJeanBeaufort.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grizzly_bear","sci":"Ursus arctos horribilis","status":null,"mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":77},
{"name":"American Black Bear","page":"American_black_bear","group":"Mammal","where":"Forests across North America, including the Florida black bear","diet":"Berries, nuts, insects, plants","facts":["Despite the name, it can be black, brown, cinnamon, blond, or even white.","It's an expert tree climber.","Most of what it eats is plants."],"lesson":"Don't judge by a name. Black bears come in half a dozen colors.","about":"The American black bear, or simply black bear, is a species of medium-sized bear which is endemic to North America. It is the continent's smallest and most widely distributed bear species. It is an omnivore, with a diet varying greatly depending on season and location.","description":"Species of bear","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0f/Vancouver_Island_black_bear_%28Ursus_americanus_vancouveri%29_Tofino_4.jpg/1920px-Vancouver_Island_black_bear_%28Ursus_americanus_vancouveri%29_Tofino_4.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Charles J. Sharp","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vancouver_Island_black_bear_(Ursus_americanus_vancouveri)_Tofino_4.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/American_black_bear","sci":"Ursus americanus","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":78},
{"name":"Honey Badger","page":"Honey_badger","group":"Mammal","where":"Africa, the Middle East and India","diet":"Almost anything: rodents, snakes, insects, honey","facts":["Its loose, thick skin lets it twist around and bite even when grabbed.","It's very resistant to many snake venoms.","It's known for taking on lions and leopards."],"lesson":"Courage beats size. The honey badger stands its ground against animals many times bigger.","about":"The honey badger, also known as the ratel, is a mammal widely distributed across Africa, Southwest Asia, and the Indian subcontinent. It is the sole living representative of the Mellivorinae subfamily of the Mustelidae.","description":"Species of mammal","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/42/Honey_Badger.jpg/1920px-Honey_Badger.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Sumeet Moghe","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Honey_Badger.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Honey_badger","sci":"Mellivora capensis","status":"LC","mass":12.0,"length":null,"life":null,"n":79},
{"name":"Wolverine","page":"Wolverine","group":"Mammal","where":"Northern forests and tundra of North America, Europe and Asia","diet":"Carrion, small mammals, roots, berries","facts":["It's the largest land member of the weasel family.","It can travel more than 30 km (20 miles) in a day through deep snow.","It can crunch frozen meat and bone with its powerful jaws."],"lesson":"Persistence goes a long way. The wolverine keeps moving through snow other animals give up on.","about":"The wolverine, also called the carcajou or quickhatch, is the largest land-dwelling member of the family Mustelidae. It is a muscular carnivore and a solitary animal. The wolverine has a reputation for ferocity and strength out of proportion to its size, with the documented ability to kill prey many times larger than itself.","description":"Species of the family Mustelidae","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/86/J%C3%A4rv1.jpg/1920px-J%C3%A4rv1.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Esquilo","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:J%C3%A4rv1.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wolverine","sci":"Gulo gulo","status":"LC","mass":14.8,"length":null,"life":null,"n":80},
{"name":"Giant Otter","page":"Giant_otter","group":"Mammal","where":"Rivers and lakes of the Amazon and Pantanal","diet":"Fish, especially piranhas and catfish","facts":["It's the longest otter, up to about 1.7 meters (5.6 feet).","Each has a unique cream-colored throat patch.","Families hunt and defend their territory together, even against caimans."],"lesson":"A loud family is a strong family. Giant otters call to each other constantly, and stick together against danger.","about":"The giant otter or giant river otter is a South American carnivorous mammal. It is the longest member of the weasel family, Mustelidae, reaching up to 1.8 m. Atypical of mustelids, the giant otter is a social species, with family groups typically supporting three to eight members.","description":"Species of mammal","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/21/Giant_otters_%28Pteronura_brasiliensis%29.jpg/1920px-Giant_otters_%28Pteronura_brasiliensis%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Charles J. Sharp","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Giant_otters_(Pteronura_brasiliensis).jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Giant_otter","sci":"Pteronura brasiliensis","status":"EN","mass":29.0,"length":null,"life":null,"n":81},
{"name":"North American River Otter","page":"North_American_river_otter","group":"Mammal","where":"Rivers, lakes and coasts of North America, including Florida","diet":"Fish, crayfish, frogs","facts":["It can hold its breath for up to about 8 minutes.","It slides down muddy and snowy banks, apparently for fun.","It closes its ears and nostrils underwater."],"lesson":"Play is part of a good life. River otters slide down banks again and again, just because.","about":"The North American river otter, also known as the northern river otter and river otter, is a semiaquatic mammal that is endemic to the North American continent throughout most of Canada and along the coasts of the United States and its inland waterways. An adult North American river otter can weigh between 5 and 14 kilograms (11–31 lb).","description":"Species of semi-aquatic mammal","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/40/Northern_River_Otter_on_Seedskadee_NWR_%2822802102984%29.jpg/1920px-Northern_River_Otter_on_Seedskadee_NWR_%2822802102984%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"USFWS Mountain-Prairie","license":"Public domain","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Northern_River_Otter_on_Seedskadee_NWR_(22802102984).jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/North_American_river_otter","sci":"Lontra canadensis","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":82},
{"name":"Black-footed Ferret","page":"Black-footed_ferret","group":"Mammal","where":"Grasslands of the western United States","diet":"Prairie dogs, about 90% of its diet","facts":["It was thought to be extinct until a ranch dog found one in Wyoming in 1981.","Every black-footed ferret alive today descends from just seven of the 18 that were rescued then.","In 2020, one called Elizabeth Ann became the first cloned endangered animal in the United States."],"lesson":"It's never too late to try. Black-footed ferrets came back from 18 survivors.","about":"The black-footed ferret, also known as the American polecat or prairie dog hunter, is a mustelid species native to central North America. It is roughly the size of a mink and is similar in appearance to the European polecat and the Asian steppe polecat. It is largely nocturnal and solitary, except when breeding or raising litters.","description":"Species of carnivore","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/cf/Mustela_nigripes_2.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"USFWS Mountain Prairie","license":"Public domain","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mustela_nigripes_2.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Black-footed_ferret","sci":"Mustela nigripes","status":"EN","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":83},
{"name":"Stoat","page":"Stoat","group":"Mammal","where":"Northern Europe, Asia and North America","diet":"Rabbits, voles, mice","facts":["In the far north its coat turns white in winter, when it's called an ermine.","It \"dances\" in leaps and twists, which may hypnotize rabbits.","It can take on prey much bigger than itself."],"lesson":"Confidence is half the battle. The stoat takes on rabbits ten times its size.","about":"The stoat, also known as the Eurasian ermine or ermine, is a species of mustelid native to Eurasia and the northern regions of North America. Because of its wide circumpolar distribution, it is listed as Least Concern on the IUCN Red List. The name ermine is used especially in its pure white winter coat of the stoat or its fur.","description":"Species of mammal","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/cf/R%C3%B8yskatt_%28Mustela_erminea_erminea%29%2C_Lista%2C_Norway.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"Marton Berntsen, crop by User:MPF","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:R%C3%B8yskatt_(Mustela_erminea_erminea),_Lista,_Norway.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stoat","sci":"Mustela erminea","status":"LC","mass":0.219,"length":0.247,"life":5.0,"n":84},
{"name":"Spotted Hyena","page":"Spotted_hyena","group":"Mammal","where":"Savannas and woodlands of Africa south of the Sahara","diet":"Wildebeest, zebras, antelope, carrion","facts":["It hunts most of its own food; it isn't just a scavenger.","Females are larger and lead the clan.","Its whooping call carries for kilometers; its famous \"giggle\" is a sign of nerves."],"lesson":"Don't believe your reputation. Hyenas are known as scavengers, yet they hunt most of what they eat.","about":"The spotted hyena, also known as the laughing hyena, is a hyena species, currently classed as the sole extant member of the genus Crocuta, native to sub-Saharan Africa. It is listed as being of least concern by the IUCN due to its widespread range and large numbers estimated between 27,000 and 47,000 individuals.","description":"Species of hyena","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dc/Spotted_hyena_%28Crocuta_crocuta%29.jpg/1920px-Spotted_hyena_%28Crocuta_crocuta%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Charles J. Sharp","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Spotted_hyena_(Crocuta_crocuta).jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spotted_hyena","sci":"Crocuta crocuta","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":85},
{"name":"Meerkat","page":"Meerkat","group":"Mammal","where":"Deserts and dry grasslands of southern Africa","diet":"Insects, scorpions, lizards","facts":["One meerkat stands guard while the others forage, and calls out a warning.","It's immune to some scorpion venom.","Dark patches around its eyes cut the desert glare."],"lesson":"Take turns looking out for each other. Meerkats eat in peace because one of them always keeps watch.","about":"The meerkat or suricate is a small mongoose found in southern Africa. It is characterised by a broad head, large eyes, a pointed snout, long legs, a thin tapering tail, and a brindled coat pattern. The head-and-body length is around 24–35 cm (9.4–13.8 in), and the weight is typically between 0.62 and 0.97 kg.","description":"Species of mongoose from Southern Africa","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9a/Meerkat_%28Suricata_suricatta%29_Tswalu.jpg/1920px-Meerkat_%28Suricata_suricatta%29_Tswalu.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Charles J. Sharp","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Meerkat_(Suricata_suricatta)_Tswalu.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Meerkat","sci":"Suricata suricatta","status":"LC","mass":0.725,"length":null,"life":null,"n":86},
{"name":"Binturong","page":"Binturong","group":"Mammal","where":"Forests of South and Southeast Asia","diet":"Fruit, especially figs; small animals","facts":["It smells like buttered popcorn.","It's one of very few mammals with a tail that can grip like a hand.","It plants fig trees by spreading their seeds."],"lesson":"Leave things better than you found them. The binturong plants forests just by eating its lunch.","about":"The binturong, also known as the bearcat, is an arboreal viverrid native to South and Southeast Asia. It is the only species in the genus Arctictis, and it is divided into nine subspecies. It has long, thick hair, and is primarily dark in appearance, but can also have a whitish speckled pelage.","description":"Asian mammal also known as a bearcat","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a7/Binturong_in_Overloon.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"Tassilo Rau","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Binturong_in_Overloon.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Binturong","sci":"Arctictis binturong","status":"VU","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":87},
{"name":"Fossa","page":"Fossa_(animal)","group":"Mammal","where":"Forests of Madagascar","diet":"Lemurs, rodents, birds","facts":["It's Madagascar's largest predator.","It looks like a cat but is related to mongooses.","It can climb down trees head first, with ankles that turn backwards."],"lesson":"You don't have to be what you look like. The fossa looks like a cat, but it's something else entirely.","about":"The fossa is a slender, long-tailed, cat-like mammal that is endemic to Madagascar. It is a member of the carnivoran family Eupleridae.","description":"Cat-like species of mammal from Madagascar","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a6/Cryptoprocta_Ferox.JPG?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"Ran Kirlian","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cryptoprocta_Ferox.JPG"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fossa_(animal)","sci":"Cryptoprocta ferox","status":"VU","mass":9.5,"length":null,"life":null,"n":88},
{"name":"White-nosed Coati","page":"White-nosed_coati","group":"Mammal","where":"Forests from Arizona to Colombia","diet":"Insects, fruit, eggs, small animals","facts":["It holds its long, ringed tail straight up as it walks.","Females and young travel in bands of up to about 30.","Its long, flexible snout sniffs out insects under leaves."],"lesson":"Follow your nose. The coati finds its food by sniffing out what others walk past.","about":"The white-nosed coati, also known as the coatimundi, is a species of coati and a member of the family Procyonidae. Local Spanish names for the species include antoon, gato solo, pizote, and tejón, depending upon the region. It weighs about 4–6 kg (8.8–13.2 lb), and the nose-to-tail length of the species is about 110 cm (3.6 ft) with about half of that being the tail length.","description":"Species of mammal","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cb/WNCoati_Alajuela_CR.png/1920px-WNCoati_Alajuela_CR.png?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Chuck Homler, Focus On Wildlife","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:WNCoati_Alajuela_CR.png"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/White-nosed_coati","sci":"Nasua narica","status":"LC","mass":0.18,"length":null,"life":null,"n":89},
{"name":"Kinkajou","page":"Kinkajou","group":"Mammal","where":"Rainforests of Central and South America","diet":"Fruit, nectar, honey","facts":["Its tongue can stretch about 13 cm (5 in) for licking nectar and honey.","Its tail can grip branches like a hand.","It can turn its back feet backward to run either way along a branch."],"lesson":"Sweetness has its place. The kinkajou lives on fruit and nectar, and pollinates flowers as it goes.","about":"The kinkajou is a mammal of the family Procyonidae, a relative to olingos, coatis, raccoons, and the ringtail and cacomistle. It is the only member of the genus Potos and is also known as the honey bear. Though kinkajous are arboreal, they are not closely related to any other tree-dwelling mammal group.","description":"South American mammal (Potos flavus)","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/31/Potos_flavus_%288973438737%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"Dick Culbert from Gibsons, B.C., Canada","license":"CC BY 2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Potos_flavus_(8973438737).jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kinkajou","sci":"Potos flavus","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":90},
{"name":"Striped Skunk","page":"Striped_skunk","group":"Mammal","where":"Most of North America","diet":"Insects, grubs, small animals, fruit","facts":["It can spray accurately about 3 meters (10 feet), and the smell carries over a kilometer.","It warns first: it stamps its feet and raises its tail before spraying.","It's one of the few animals that eats honey bees, hive and all."],"lesson":"Give a warning before you strike back. The skunk always stamps and hisses first.","about":"The striped skunk is a skunk of the genus Mephitis that occurs across much of North America, including southern Canada, the United States, and northern Mexico. It is currently listed as least concern by the IUCN on account of its wide range and ability to adapt to human-modified environments.","description":"Species of mammal","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1f/Skunk_about_to_spray.jpg/1920px-Skunk_about_to_spray.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Wallace Keck","license":"Public domain","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Skunk_about_to_spray.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Striped_skunk","sci":"Mephitis mephitis","status":"LC","mass":0.034,"length":null,"life":null,"n":91},
{"name":"European Badger","page":"European_badger","group":"Mammal","where":"Woodlands and fields across Europe","diet":"Earthworms, insects, fruit, roots","facts":["Families live in \"setts,\" underground tunnels that can be used for centuries.","It can eat hundreds of earthworms in a night.","It changes its bedding regularly, dragging in fresh grass and leaves."],"lesson":"Build something that lasts. Badger setts are handed down for generations.","about":"The European badger, also known as the Eurasian badger, is a species of badger in the family Mustelidae native to Europe and West Asia and parts of Central Asia. It is classified as least concern on the IUCN Red List due to its wide range and large, stable population size which is thought to be increasing in some regions. Several subspecies are recognised, with the nominate subspecies predominating in most of Europe.","description":"Species of mustelid","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1a/European_badger_%28Meles_meles_taxus%29_Drenthe.jpg/1920px-European_badger_%28Meles_meles_taxus%29_Drenthe.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Charles J. Sharp","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:European_badger_(Meles_meles_taxus)_Drenthe.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/European_badger","sci":"Meles meles","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":92},
{"name":"Asian Small-clawed Otter","page":"Asian_small-clawed_otter","group":"Mammal","where":"Wetlands and rivers of South and Southeast Asia","diet":"Crabs, shellfish, fish","facts":["It's the smallest otter.","Its nimble, partly webbed paws can feel for crabs under stones.","Families are close and chatty, with over a dozen different calls."],"lesson":"Use the tools you have. Small-clawed otters feel their way to food with nimble hands.","about":"The Asian small-clawed otter, also called oriental small-clawed otter and small-clawed otter, is an otter species native to South and Southeast Asia. It is the only species of the subgenus Amblonyx in the genus Lutra. It has short claws that do not extend beyond the pads of its webbed digits.","description":"Species of mammal","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/14/Otter_-_melbourne_zoo.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"fir0002 flagstaffotos [at] gmail.com Canon 20D + Canon 70-200mm f/2.8 L","license":"GFDL 1.2","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Otter_-_melbourne_zoo.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Asian_small-clawed_otter","sci":"Aonyx cinerea","status":null,"mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":93}
